# Repo Anti-Rot — dashboard environment.
#
# Every variable here is OPTIONAL: with none set the app runs, scans and serves
# the dashboard. They exist to lock things down and to enable the paid-for extras.
# Copy to `.env.local` for local use, or paste into your host's env settings.
#
# One rule worth reading before deploying publicly: the defaults are tuned for a
# laptop, not for the open internet. The scan limits below are the ones to change.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public scan limits  (lib/scan-limits.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api/scan clones and scans arbitrary repositories on your machine — minutes of
# CPU and hundreds of MB of IO per request. These bound the damage.
#
# NOTE: the limiter keeps state in process memory. Run more than one instance and
# each gets its own budget, so the effective limit multiplies by instance count.

# Scans allowed per client per window.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_PER_WINDOW=30
# Length of that window, in seconds.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_WINDOW_S=3600
# Repositories cloned+scanned at once, process-wide. This is the setting that
# actually protects CPU, memory and disk — set it to 1 on a 512 MB instance.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_CONCURRENCY=2
# Heap ceiling for the scanner child process, in MB. Without one, a large
# repository grows the child until the CONTAINER runs out of memory and the
# platform kills the whole service — every request in flight dies and the
# instance restarts. With one, the child dies alone and the caller gets an
# explanation. Default 320, tuned for a 512 MB box; raise it on a bigger one.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_HEAP_MB=320
# Callers allowed to wait for a slot before requests are refused outright.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_QUEUE=10
# How long a caller may wait for a slot before giving up, in seconds.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_QUEUE_WAIT_S=60
# URLs accepted per request. Set to 1 for a public single-repo diagnostic.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_MAX_URLS=20
# Proxy hops you control, for reading x-forwarded-for. Almost every PaaS puts
# exactly one proxy in front, so 1 is right. Raise it ONLY if you added your own
# proxy/CDN in front of that — too high a value trusts a forged client address.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Share-link storage  (lib/share-store.ts)  — optional but strongly advised
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# With these unset, share links are written to the container filesystem. That is
# fine locally and WRONG on most hosts: a container disk is ephemeral, and on
# Render's free tier there is no persistent disk at all, so a redeploy silently
# breaks every link anyone has already posted. Configure these before announcing
# anything publicly.
#
# Run once in the Supabase SQL editor:
#
#   create table public.shared_reports (
#     token       text primary key,
#     created_at  timestamptz not null default now(),
#     report      jsonb not null
#   );
#   alter table public.shared_reports enable row level security;
#
# Add no RLS policies. With none, the anon and authenticated roles can do
# nothing, and only the service role below — which bypasses RLS and never leaves
# the server — can read or write. The share token is the capability; the database
# is not meant to be a second, weaker gate.
#
# Stable shares (one URL per repo, updatable snapshot) store a small envelope
# inside the existing `report` jsonb — no ALTER TABLE. Free Render has no
# persistent disk: leave these unset and every redeploy wipes share links.

# Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefgh.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=
# Service role key (Settings → API → service_role). This bypasses row-level
# security: it is a server secret. Never expose it to the browser, never prefix
# it with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and rotate it if it ever lands in a client bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operator key  (lib/owner.ts)  — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exempts YOU from the scan limits above without loosening them for everybody.
# Set any long random string, then paste it once into Settings → Operator key in
# your browser. It is stored in an httpOnly cookie, so no page script can read
# it back, and it is checked in constant time.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# What it lifts: the per-hour scan allowance, and REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_MAX_URLS.
# What it does NOT lift: REPO_ANTI_ROT_SCAN_CONCURRENCY. That one is not an
# abuse control — it is what stops two simultaneous clones exhausting the memory
# of a small instance. Bypassing it would not buy you throughput, it would buy
# you an out-of-memory kill in the middle of your own scan.
#
# Unset means nobody is the owner — never "everybody is". Treat it like a
# password: anyone holding it can spend your machine without limit.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_OWNER_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub preview cards  (app/api/github/route.ts)  — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scan form previews a repository — stars, language, licence, last push —
# and searches GitHub when you type a name instead of a URL. It works with this
# unset, but only just: GitHub allows 60 lookups an hour and 10 searches a minute
# from an unauthenticated address, counted against the SERVER's address, so on a
# public deploy every visitor shares that budget. Answers are cached for ten
# minutes, which is what makes the unauthenticated case usable at all.
#
# A classic (or fine-grained) token with NO scopes raises this to 5000/hour. No
# scopes is deliberate: this only ever reads public repositories, and a token
# that can do more is a token that can be misused if it leaks.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secrets for the CI → dashboard loop. Unset means the endpoint is open,
# which is fine locally and wrong on a public host.

# Required as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to POST a report to /api/ingest.
# Set the same value as the GitHub Action's `dashboard-token` input.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_INGEST_TOKEN=
# Required to GET /api/reports. Separate from the ingest token on purpose:
# writing reports and reading everyone's reports are different privileges.
#
# ALSO required to GET /api/stats. Note the different rule there: unset means the
# stats endpoint returns 503, not "open". Everywhere else an unset token disables
# a check for local convenience; for an endpoint that serves everybody's activity
# that default would be a leak, so it fails closed instead.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_READ_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Usage statistics  (lib/usage.ts)  — optional, needs the Supabase config above
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Counts how much the service is used: one row per scan, per commit-scan, per
# share link created and per shared report opened. Each row holds a random
# per-browser id, the event name, the repository host and owner/name, a count and
# whether it succeeded — and nothing else. No findings, no scores, no file paths,
# no IP addresses, no user agents. Visitors can switch it off in Settings.
#
# A scan also writes a row to scan_stats (below), which is the other half of the
# same switch. Between them the split is: this table knows WHICH repositories are
# scanned and not how they did; that one knows how they did and not which they
# were. Neither can answer both questions, which is the point.
#
# There is no separate variable: with SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# unset, recording is a no-op, so a self-hosted instance collects nothing by
# default. To enable it, run once in the Supabase SQL editor:
#
#   create table public.usage_events (
#     id       bigint generated always as identity primary key,
#     at       timestamptz not null default now(),
#     visitor  text not null,
#     event    text not null,
#     host     text,
#     repo     text,
#     amount   integer not null default 1,
#     ok       boolean
#   );
#   create index usage_events_at_idx on public.usage_events (at desc);
#   alter table public.usage_events enable row level security;
#
# Same policy as shared_reports: RLS on with no policies, so only the service
# role reaches it. Read the numbers with
#
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $REPO_ANTI_ROT_READ_TOKEN" \
#        https://your-host/api/stats?days=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Score distribution  (lib/scan-stats.ts)  — optional, same Supabase config
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What makes "worse than 71% of comparable repositories" possible. One row per
# successful scan, holding the SHAPE of the result and nothing that identifies
# the project: score, grade, the main language, a size band, the count of
# findings per severity, and whether CI and a test runner were detected.
#
# Note what the table has no column for: owner, name, host, url, visitor,
# commit. Not "we choose not to write them" — there is nowhere to put them.
# `day` is a date rather than a timestamp so that a row here cannot be lined up
# against a usage_events row by the second. On a day with a single scan somebody
# holding both tables could still guess, and that is the honest limit of it.
#
# Governed by the same Settings switch as usage statistics; unset Supabase means
# no distribution, and no percentile is shown to anyone.
#
#   create table public.scan_stats (
#     id        bigint generated always as identity primary key,
#     day       date not null,
#     score     smallint not null,
#     grade     text not null,
#     language  text,
#     size      text not null,
#     critical  integer not null default 0,
#     warning   integer not null default 0,
#     info      integer not null default 0,
#     ci        boolean not null default false,
#     tests     boolean not null default false
#   );
#   create index scan_stats_day_idx on public.scan_stats (day desc);
#   create index scan_stats_cut_idx on public.scan_stats (language, size);
#   alter table public.scan_stats enable row level security;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI layer  (app/api/ai/complete)  — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without these the dashboard still works; users can paste their own OpenRouter
# key in Settings and pay for their own usage.

# A shared server-side key, so visitors get AI features without pasting one.
# THIS SPENDS YOUR CREDITS.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Required whenever OPENROUTER_API_KEY is set. Callers must send it as
# `Authorization: Bearer <token>` to use the shared key. Without it the route
# returns 503 rather than letting anonymous visitors spend your balance —
# set both or neither.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_AI_PROXY_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated allowlist for the shared key, e.g.
# "anthropic/claude-3.5-haiku,openai/gpt-4o-mini". Empty means any model, which
# also means any price.
OPENROUTER_ALLOWED_MODELS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Score-drop alerts  (lib/webhook.ts)  — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fired from /api/ingest when a repo's score falls versus the previous scan.

# Incoming webhook URL (Slack, Discord, Mattermost — anything taking {"text": …}).
REPO_ANTI_ROT_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Minimum score drop before alerting. 1 = any drop at all.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_WEBHOOK_MIN_DROP=1
# Public dashboard URL, appended to the alert so the link is clickable.
REPO_ANTI_ROT_DASHBOARD_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deprecated spellings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The four variables above once used a `RAR_` prefix — RAR_AI_PROXY_TOKEN,
# RAR_WEBHOOK_URL, RAR_WEBHOOK_MIN_DROP, RAR_DASHBOARD_URL. Two prefixes for one
# project meant you had to read the source to know which applied where.
#
# The old names still resolve (lib/env.ts) and there is no rush to change them.
# They are only read when the new name is unset, so setting both is safe.
